# Risk-acceptance file for the production image Trivy scan (release.yaml).
# Every entry below must have a comment explaining WHY it's accepted and
# which class it belongs to. See SECURITY.md > Container Security for context.
#
# Classes:
#   1) PRUNE CANDIDATE   — not required by FluidSender or the base OS's
#                          package manager; a good target for `apt-get purge`
#                          in ui/Dockerfile.prd. Accepted temporarily (short
#                          expiry) until that removal work happens.
#                          Currently EMPTY: every package initially suspected
#                          of being prunable (perl-base, ncurses-base,
#                          ncurses-bin, libtinfo6) turned out to be Debian
#                          Essential — verified experimentally via
#                          `apt-get purge`, which refused all four. See
#                          Class 3 below.
#   2) REQUIRED          — a package FluidSender's own code depends on at
#                          runtime (currently: udev, for serial port
#                          enumeration via @serialport/bindings-cpp). Accepted
#                          because we need the package itself, not because the
#                          CVE is harmless.
#   3) OS-ESSENTIAL       — Debian `Priority: required`/`Essential: yes`
#                          packages that dpkg/apt need to function. apt
#                          refuses to remove these without
#                          --allow-remove-essential, which risks breaking the
#                          image outright. Not removable without switching to
#                          a non-Debian base image (tracked separately).
#
# Node.js/npm findings (expr-eval, picomatch, sigstore) are intentionally NOT
# listed here — those are FluidSender's own dependency tree, not the base
# image, and should be fixed by bumping/replacing the package, not accepted.

# --- Class 3: OS-essential (Debian required/essential packages) ---

# util-linux family (bsdutils, libblkid1, libmount1, libsmartcols1, libuuid1,
# util-linux, util-linux-extra): Priority: required / Essential: yes.
# apt-get refuses removal without --allow-remove-essential.
CVE-2026-53615 exp:2027-01-10

# gzip: Priority: required, used by dpkg itself to unpack .deb archives.
CVE-2026-41992 exp:2027-01-10

# libacl1: dependency of the essential util-linux family above.
CVE-2026-54369 exp:2027-01-10

# zlib1g: Debian status is will_not_fix (minizip codepath, not reachable via
# normal zlib usage). Also a hard dependency of dpkg/apt.
CVE-2023-45853 exp:2027-01-10

# libtinfo6/ncurses-base/ncurses-bin: looked like a prune candidate (only
# backs bash's terminal handling) but is NOT removable — verified via
# `apt-get purge` in ui/Dockerfile.prd / ui/Dockerfile: libtinfo6 is a hard
# PreDepends of both bash and util-linux, and util-linux is Debian-essential
# (see CVE-2026-53615 above). apt refuses the whole transaction rather than
# cascade an essential-package removal.
CVE-2025-69720 exp:2027-01-10

# perl-base: also looked prunable but is itself a Debian Essential package —
# apt refused with "Essential packages were removed and -y was used without
# --allow-remove-essential", confirmed experimentally. Never invoked by
# FluidSender, but not removable without forcing an essential-package
# removal, which risks breaking the image outright.
CVE-2026-42496 exp:2027-01-10
CVE-2026-8376 exp:2027-01-10
CVE-2026-42497 exp:2027-01-10
CVE-2026-48962 exp:2027-01-10
CVE-2026-9538 exp:2027-01-10
